window.__labLoaded({"id":"1.4","title":"Histograms, Shape and Skewness","goal":"Use the lesson's 20 exam scores to tally a histogram, compare mean and median, and measure skewness.","question":"A teacher has 20 exam scores. The mean is 65.7 and the median 65.5, which sounds like an ordinary class. Before she trusts the average she wants to see the shape: are the scores balanced, or stretched out by a few very high ones?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"Mean versus median","text":"The lesson's 20 exam scores. When the mean sits above the median, a tail is pulling it to the right, which hints at right skew.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\nscores = np.array([42, 48, 51, 55, 57, 58, 61, 62, 63, 65, 66, 67, 68, 70, 72, 73, 75, 79, 84, 98])\n\nprint(f'mean   = {scores.mean():.1f}')\nprint(f'median = {np.median(scores):.1f}')","explain":["Load NumPy, the library for lists of numbers, as np.","Load SciPy's statistics tools; we need stats.skew in step 3.","Load Matplotlib's pyplot as plt, for the histogram in step 4.","Store the 20 exam scores in an array.","Print the mean, the balance point of the scores.","Print the median. If the mean sits above the median, a right tail may be pulling it."],"meaning":"The mean is 65.7 and the median 65.5, only 0.2 apart. By this test the class is close to balanced, with at most a slight pull to the right. The histogram and a skewness number will show the shape more clearly.","out":"mean   = 65.7\nmedian = 65.5","figs":[]},{"title":"Tally the bins","text":"np.histogram counts how many scores fall in each bin. Bins of width 10 starting at 40 match the lesson. A bin includes its lower edge, and the counts must add up to 20.","code":"edges = np.arange(40, 101, 10)\ncounts, _ = np.histogram(scores, bins=edges)\n\nfor lo, c in zip(edges[:-1], counts):\n    print(f'{lo} to {lo + 9}: {c}')\nprint('total =', counts.sum())","explain":["Bin edges 40, 50, ... 100. The stop value 101 is not included, so 100 is the last edge. A width of 10 matches the lesson.","Count the scores in each bin. A bin includes its lower edge. The underscore ignores the edges, which we already have.","Walk through the bins together with their counts.","Print each bin as 'start to end: count'.","Print the total. It must equal 20, otherwise some score fell outside the bins."],"meaning":"The counts are 2, 4, 7, 5, 1 and 1, and they add up to 20, so every score is in a bin. The tallest bin is 60 to 69 with 7 students. The right side stretches further from the centre than the left: the top score (98) is about 33 above the median, while the lowest (42) is about 24 below.","out":"40 to 49: 2\n50 to 59: 4\n60 to 69: 7\n70 to 79: 5\n80 to 89: 1\n90 to 99: 1\ntotal = 20","figs":[]},{"title":"A number for skewness","text":"stats.skew measures lopsidedness. The default (bias=True) is the plain population formula. bias=False applies the small-sample correction that Excel and SPSS use, and that the lesson quotes (0.53). A positive value means a longer right tail, and mirroring the data flips the sign.","code":"print(f'skew, default formula   = {stats.skew(scores):.3f}')\nprint(f'skew, sample-corrected   = {stats.skew(scores, bias=False):.3f}')\nmirror = 100 - scores\nprint(f'mirrored (left-skewed)   = {stats.skew(mirror, bias=False):.3f}')","explain":["The default stats.skew uses the plain population formula (bias=True).","bias=False adds the small-sample correction used by Excel and SPSS, and quoted in the lesson.","Mirror the data by taking 100 minus each score. This turns a right tail into a left tail.","Print the skewness of the mirrored data, to see that the sign flips."],"meaning":"Skewness is 0.486 with the plain formula and 0.527 with the small-sample correction (the figure the lesson quotes). Positive means a longer right tail. The mirrored data give -0.527, a left-skewed shape. As a rough guide, values between -0.5 and 0.5 are near symmetric, so 0.53 is a mild right skew.","out":"skew, default formula   = 0.486\nskew, sample-corrected   = 0.527\nmirrored (left-skewed)   = -0.527","figs":[]},{"title":"Draw the histogram","text":"The bars touch because the scores sit on one number line. The long tail on the right (84 and 98) is easy to see. The dashed line marks the mean and the solid line the median.","code":"plt.figure(figsize=(6, 3.4))\nplt.hist(scores, bins=edges, edgecolor='white')\nplt.axvline(scores.mean(), linestyle='--', color='black', label='mean')\nplt.axvline(np.median(scores), color='black', label='median')\nplt.xlabel('exam score')\nplt.ylabel('number of students')\nplt.legend()\nplt.show()","explain":["Open a new blank figure, 6 by 3.4 inches.","Draw the histogram with our bin edges. The white edges make the bars easy to tell apart.","Add a dashed vertical line at the mean.","Add a solid vertical line at the median.","Label the horizontal axis with what the numbers are.","Label the vertical axis: each bar counts students.","Add a legend, so the reader knows which line is the mean and which is the median.","Show the figure."],"meaning":"The bars touch because scores sit on one number line. The tallest bar is 60 to 69, and the bars thin out slowly toward the right (84 and 98), so the right tail is longer. The dashed mean line lies almost on top of the solid median line, which agrees that the skew is only mild.","out":"","figs":["1.4-4.png"]}]},"r":{"code":"x <- c(42, 48, 51, 55, 57, 58, 61, 62, 63, 65, 66, 67, 68, 70, 72, 73, 75, 79, 84, 98)\nmean(x); median(x)\nhist(x, breaks = seq(40, 100, by = 10), right = FALSE)\nn <- length(x); m <- mean(x)\ng1 <- (sum((x - m)^3) / n) / (sum((x - m)^2) / n)^1.5\ng1\ng1 * sqrt(n * (n - 1)) / (n - 2)","explain":["Store the 20 exam scores in a vector.","Two commands on one line, separated by a semicolon: the mean and then the median.","Draw the histogram with bins from 40 to 100 in steps of 10. right = FALSE makes each bin include its lower edge, as in Python.","Store n and the mean in two variables, to keep the next formula short.","Skewness g1: the average cubed deviation divided by the SD cubed. Cubing keeps the sign, so the tail direction shows.","Print g1.","Multiply by sqrt(n(n - 1)) / (n - 2), the small-sample correction used by SPSS and Excel."],"expect":"mean 65.7; median 65.5; skewness g1 0.486, sample-corrected G1 0.527; histogram bar heights 2, 4, 7, 5, 1, 1","read":"R prints 65.7 and 65.5, a histogram with bars of height 2, 4, 7, 5, 1, 1, then g1 = 0.486 and the corrected value 0.527. A positive skewness means a tail to the right, here a mild one.","note":"Base R has no skewness function, so the formula is written out. The first value matches Python's default (0.486) and the second matches bias=False (0.527)."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Frequencies > Statistics and Charts","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / score.\nBEGIN DATA\n42  48  51  55  57  58  61  62  63  65  66  67  68  70  72  73  75  79  84  98\nEND DATA.\nFREQUENCIES VARIABLES=score\n  /FORMAT=NOTABLE\n  /STATISTICS=MEAN MEDIAN SKEWNESS SESKEWNESS\n  /HISTOGRAM.","explain":["Tell SPSS we type the data in the syntax, in free format, into one variable called score.","BEGIN DATA starts the data lines.","The 20 exam scores, separated by spaces.","END DATA ends the data lines.","Frequencies is the command that can give statistics and a histogram.","NOTABLE hides the long table listing every value.","Ask for the mean, median, skewness and the standard error of the skewness.","Ask for a histogram, and finish the command with a full stop."],"read":"In the Statistics table read Mean 65.70, Median 65.50, Skewness 0.527 and Std. Error of Skewness 0.512. The skewness is only about one standard error above 0, so it is not clearly different from a symmetric shape. The histogram shows the same mild right tail.","note":"SPSS reports the sample-corrected skewness, 0.527, the same as bias=False. Its histogram picks its own bin widths, so it will not look exactly like the 10-point bins."},"stata":{"code":"clear\nset obs 20\ngenerate score = real(word(\"42  48  51  55  57  58  61  62  63  65  66  67  68  70  72  73  75  79  84  98\", _n))\nsummarize score, detail\ndisplay r(skewness) * sqrt(r(N) * (r(N) - 1)) / (r(N) - 2)\nhistogram score, width(10) start(40) frequency","explain":["Clear any old data from memory.","Create 20 empty rows.","Fill score row by row: word() picks the n-th number and real() makes it numeric.","summarize with detail prints the mean, the median (50% row) and the skewness.","Apply the small-sample correction to Stata's skewness. r(skewness) and r(N) are stored by summarize.","Draw the histogram with bins 10 wide starting at 40, showing counts on the vertical axis."],"read":"summarize shows Mean 65.7, the 50% row 65.5 (the median) and Skewness .486. The display line applies the correction and prints about .527. The histogram has bars 10 wide from 40, with a slightly longer tail on the right.","note":"summarize, detail prints Skewness 0.486, the same as Python's default. The display line applies the small-sample correction to get 0.527. histogram draws 10-point bins from 40."},"conclusion":"The 20 scores are close to symmetric with a mild right tail: the mean (65.7) and median (65.5) are nearly equal, the skewness is +0.53 and the histogram stretches a little further toward the high scores (84 and 98). Mean and SD give a fair summary here. Caution: with only 20 scores the shape is uncertain, and a different bin width can change how the histogram looks.","practice":{"scenario":"A city planner records the commute time (in minutes) of 25 workers. Most commute under 40 minutes, but a few travel much longer. She wants to know whether to describe the typical commute with the mean or the median.","data":"commute = [12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 23, 25, 26, 27, 28, 30, 31, 33, 35, 36, 38, 40, 44, 47, 52, 58, 65, 75, 90, 110]","tasks":["Find the mean and median of the commute times.","Count the commutes in 20-minute bins from 0 to 120.","Compute the sample-corrected skewness.","Name the shape and say which average you would report."],"hint":"Use np.histogram with bins=np.arange(0, 121, 20) and stats.skew(data, bias=False). A mean above the median points to a right tail.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\ncommute = np.array([12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 23, 25, 26, 27, 28, 30, 31, 33, 35, 36, 38, 40, 44, 47, 52, 58, 65, 75, 90, 110])\nprint(f'n = {len(commute)}, mean = {commute.mean():.1f}, median = {np.median(commute):.1f}')\ncounts, _ = np.histogram(commute, bins=np.arange(0, 121, 20))\nprint('counts per 20-minute bin:', counts)\nprint(f'skewness (sample-corrected) = {stats.skew(commute, bias=False):.2f}')","answer":"The mean is 40.0 minutes but the median is 33.0, a gap of 7. The bin counts are 3, 13, 5, 2, 1, 1: 13 of 25 commutes fall in the 20 to 39 bin, with a long thin tail up to 110. Skewness is 1.51, clearly right-skewed. Report the median, 33 minutes, as the typical commute and mention the tail.","out":"n = 25, mean = 40.0, median = 33.0\ncounts per 20-minute bin: [ 3 13  5  2  1  1]\nskewness (sample-corrected) = 1.51","figs":[]}}});
